#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443 :topic/difficulty 1.2233896690425385 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428224 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1361->30. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} :encompassing/weight 0.5}] :topic/id #uuid "f580d418-6860-5b04-b964-2999aa08b42f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1361 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754}] :topic/title "Integrating the Reciprocal Function"}]}
